  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 151 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThe ridge axis that nosed up into the north central US will be \npinched off as shortwave energy starts to flow into the region. \nThese pieces of energy will be coupled with waves of low level \nthermal lift, weak to modest QG convergence surges, and a nocturnal \nlow level jet pointed into eastern Nebraska or Iowa from Thursday \ninto at least Saturday. All these factors point to an active period \nof wet conditions featuring showers and occasional storms with \nshort spans of dry conditions. Atmospheric moisture will also \ntick upward from Thursday with precipitable water values back \nabove 1.5 inches, which will be more common than not through \nSaturday evening. While warm cloud depths and precipitable water\nvalues will not be as high as last week, they will still be \nwell above normal for the latter part of September and favor \nefficient rainfall production and slow movement given the 850-300mb\nflow between 20 and 30 knots. Rainfall totals from the global \nmodels are not eye popping at 1 to 3 inches Thursday through \nSunday morning with ensemble probabilities less than 20% for 1\" \nof rainfall in a 24 hour period. However, given antecedent wet \nconditions (National Water Model analysis showing 60% or higher \nsoil moisture, NASA SPoRT top 4\" and 16\" showing 70th percentile\nor high soil moisture south of Iowa 3) and elevated streamflows\nin all but the upper Iowa River basin and the upper forks of \nthe Des Moines River, will need to monitor rainfall trends for \nany flash flooding or additional rivers rises later this week \ninto the weekend. Lapse rates during this period will be weak \nand any amount of instability and favorable shear do not overlap\ntemporally so while there could be a few strong storms, no \nwidespread severe weather is foreseen. This is further supported\nby AI/ML guidance, which is not pinging on anything late this \nweek and weekend.",
